<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    GeneralsX Loader - the thing a friend actually double-clicks.

    GeneralsX @feature Claude 08/08/2026

    SELF-CONTAINED ON PURPOSE. A friend must not be told to install a .NET runtime before they can
    install a game; the whole point of this program is that it is the only thing they have to run.
    That costs about 70 MB in the zip and buys a support conversation that never happens.

    WinForms rather than WPF: this is five controls and a progress bar. WPF would add XAML, a
    designer and a larger trimming surface for no user-visible difference.

    PublishSingleFile with IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract so the result is ONE file. A folder
    of 200 DLLs beside the exe reads as malware to a normal person, and they are right to hesitate.

    NOT trimmed: WinForms uses reflection all over its data binding, and a trimmed WinForms app
    fails at runtime in ways that do not show up at build time. This is not a place to save 20 MB.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <UseWindowsForms>true</UseWindowsForms>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>

    <AssemblyName>GeneralsXLauncher</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>GeneralsX.Loader</RootNamespace>
    <Version>1.0.0</Version>
    <Product>GeneralsX Launcher</Product>
    <Company>GeneralsX</Company>
    <Description>GeneralsX Launcher - installs and updates GeneralsX without touching your Zero Hour installation.</Description>

    <!--
      Lets this project be BUILT from macOS/Linux (CI, or a dev box with no Windows). It only
      downloads the Windows targeting packs; the produced exe is identical either way. Without it
      the SDK refuses with NETSDK1100 on any non-Windows host, which would make the CI job below
      Windows-only for no reason.
    -->
    <EnableWindowsTargeting>true</EnableWindowsTargeting>

    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <SelfContained>true</SelfContained>
    <PublishSingleFile>true</PublishSingleFile>
    <IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>true</IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>
    <PublishTrimmed>false</PublishTrimmed>
    <EnableCompressionInSingleFile>true</EnableCompressionInSingleFile>
    <SatelliteResourceLanguages>en</SatelliteResourceLanguages>

    <!--
      asInvoker, and it is not decoration. Windows' installer-detection heuristic elevates a
      legacy-looking executable, and an unsigned program asking for administrator is exactly the
      shape people are right to refuse. Everything this program writes lives under the user's own
      %LOCALAPPDATA% and Documents. There is nothing here to elevate for.
    -->
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>

    <!--
      The game's own icon, copied from GeneralsMD/Code/Main/Generals.ico. Copied rather than
      referenced across the repo: this project is built standalone on a Windows box and by CI, and a
      ../../../.. path into the engine tree breaks the moment either is built from a different root.
      It is 15 KB and it is the difference between a friend seeing a recognisable C&C icon in their
      downloads folder and seeing a generic blank exe they are right to be suspicious of.
    -->
    <ApplicationIcon>GeneralsX.ico</ApplicationIcon>

    <!-- The single-file host has no place to put a .pdb a friend would ever read. -->
    <DebugType>none</DebugType>
    <!--
      GeneralsX @refactor Claude 19/08/2026 THIS PROJECT NOW HAS NO THIRD-PARTY UI DEPENDENCY.
      MetroModernUI was the only one, and the NU1701 justification that stood here - it ships .NET
      Framework 4.x assemblies consumed through the compatibility shim - went with it. Two warnings
      per build disappear as a side effect.

      It was removed for MetroCheckBox, which cannot be made DPI-correct: a 12px hardcoded tick box
      and a label font built in PIXEL units inside OnPaint, where Control.Font and ScaleControl
      cannot reach it. The rest of what the library still contributed was unwanted - see the note in
      LauncherForm's constructor.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn)</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      One real frame of the game, behind the HUD previews on the Options > HUD page. See
      HudGround.cs for where it came from and why it is 33 KB rather than a screenshot fetched
      from the CDN like the ones in Previews.cs.

      EMBEDDED, NOT FETCHED, AND THAT IS THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT Previews.cs ARGUES FOR - on purpose.
      Those previews are per-option data that changes when an archive changes, so shipping them in
      the exe would make correcting one screenshot cost every player the whole runtime tier. This
      one is not data: it is the backdrop the size arithmetic is judged against, it never changes
      when an archive does, and a preview of "is this text legible over terrain" that says
      "unavailable offline" has failed at the only question it exists to answer.

      LogicalName is set rather than inherited so the lookup in HudGround.cs does not depend on the
      SDK's RootNamespace-plus-filename rule staying what it is.
    -->
    <EmbeddedResource Include="HudGround.jpg" LogicalName="GeneralsX.Loader.HudGround.jpg" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <!--
    THE PREVIEW ART, INSIDE THE EXE.

    GeneralsX @feature Claude 17/08/2026

    Previews used to be fetched, and the argument for that was good: a picture is data, and this
    project's founding rule is that data must not need a client release. It was still the wrong
    trade. Fetched art is absent exactly when somebody is looking at the options screen without a
    connection, and "Preview unavailable offline" was the ONLY sentence five of five preview boxes
    ever displayed. The network path survives as a strictly additive upgrade - it can fill in art
    for an option this build has none for, so a preview for a NEWLY shipped archive is still a
    publish rather than a release - but the picture a player sees by default now ships with the
    program and cannot go missing.

    WHAT IT COSTS, measured rather than estimated: the one screenshot is 348,076 bytes. The launcher
    rides the ~63.5 MB runtime payload tier, so that is about half a percent of a tier every player
    already downloads, and EnableCompressionInSingleFile is on. A JPEG does not compress further, so
    treat 340 KB as the real number. If this directory ever approaches a few megabytes the answer is
    NOT to go back to fetching - it is to keep one base per aspect ratio and transparent layers for
    the rest, which is what the layer format is for: a layer is the changed region, not a frame.

    LogicalName IS SET EXPLICITLY and must stay that way. MSBuild's default resource naming mangles
    a filename into an identifier-ish form, and these names begin with '!' and contain several dots
    because they are named after overlay archives. A resource whose name is not the one the code
    asks for is not a build error - it is a picture that silently never appears.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="previews\**\*.png;previews\**\*.jpg;previews\**\*.txt">
      <LogicalName>gxpreview/%(Filename)%(Extension)</LogicalName>
    </EmbeddedResource>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
